speechtext:
Very well. The citation from the Journals of 1882 is as follows:
The question being again proposed that the bill be now read a second time-
That was the main motion 'before the house- -Mr. Mills moved, in amendment, seconded by Mr. Rymal, that all the words after "that" to the end of the question be left out, and the words, "in readjusting the representation of the people in parliament, there should be no needless interference with existing arrangements. and the principle of justice and fair

Plebiscite Act-Point of Order
play toward both political parties should be observed; that the bill contains numerous needless changes, and is framed with a view to concentrate large bodies of the Liberal voters i:n a few electoral districts, and to add Conservative voters to, or remove Liberal voters from, many electoral districts, and thus to weaken the effective strength of the Liberal party, and to pack the constituencies of Ontario in favour of the Conservatives" inserted instead thereof.
That amendment, I must say, has a familiar ring to it.' That question was in order, was put, and was defeated.
Then, if Your Honour will refer to the Journals of the British House of Commons, volume 146, for the years 1890 and 1891, you will find this, at page 303:
The order of the day being read for the second reading of the Newfoundland Fisheries bill;
And a motion being made, and the question being proposed, that the bill be now read a second time; And a debate arising thereupon;
An amendment was proposed to be made to the question by leaving out from the word "that", to the end of the question, in order to add the words "this house, having been informed that a satisfactory act has now passed the legislature of Newfoundland, and declaring its readiness to support the government in baking all measures necessary for carrying out the treaty obligations of this country, and the arrangements for arbitration made with the government of France in this matter, does not now proceed to the second reading of the bill," instead thereof. And the question being put, that the words proposed to be left out stand part of the question:-It passed in the negative.
And the question being put, that the words "this house, having been informed that a satisfactory act has now passed the legislature of Newfoundland, and declaring its readiness to support the government in taking all measures necessary for carrying out the treaty obligations of this country, and the arrangements for arbitration made with the government of France in this matter, does not now proceed to the second reading of the bill" be added to the word "That" in the original question: It was resolved in the affirmative.
And the main question, so amended, being put;
Resolved, that this house, having been informed that a satisfactory act has now passed the legislature of Newfoundland, and declaring its readiness to support the government in taking all measures necessary for carrying out the treaty obligations of this country, and the arrangements for arbitration made with the government of France in this matter, does not now proceed to the second reading of the bill.
That, surely, is some authority. Then I quote from the Journals of the British House of Commons of 1859:
The order of the day being read, for the second reading of the Representation of the People Bill;
And a motion being made, and the question being proposed, that the bill be now read a second time;
An amendment was proposed to be made to the question, by leaving out from the word
"That" to the end of the question, in order to add the words "this house is of opinion, that it is neither just nor politic to interfere, in the manner proposed in this bill, with the freehold franchise as hitherto exercised in the counties in England and Wales; and that no readjustment of the franchise will satisfy this house or the country, which does not provide for a greater extension of the suffrage in cities and boroughs than is contemplated in the present measure," instead thereof.
I would refer Your Honour to two specific precedents in Canada. I refer first to the Journals of the House of Commons, volume 19, 1885, pages 30S and 309, April 16 and 17, 1885;
The order of the day being read for the second reading of the bill respecting the electoral franchise:
And the question being proposed, that the bill be now read a second time;
Sir Richard J. Cartwright moved in amendment, seconded by Mr. Mackenzie, that all the words after "That", to the end of the question, be left out, and the words "in the opinion of this house, it is not possible at this late stage of the session, and having regard to the present condition of the public business, to discuss the said bill satisfactorily," inserted instead thereaf;
And a debate arising thereupon;
And the house having continued to sit till after twelve of the clock on Friday morning;
And the question being put on the amendment; the house divided: and the names being called for they were taken down, as follows.
So it passed in the negative.
It was quite in order to do that, and in principle this is the same as that.
I refer Your Honour to the Journals of this house, volume 19, 1885, pages 93 and 94, February 19, 1885:
The order of the day being read, for the second reading of the bill to limit the appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court;
Mr. Landry (Montmagny) moved, seconded by Mr. McMillan (Vaudreuil), and the question being proposed, that the bill be now read a second time;
Mr. Ouimet moved in amendment, seconded by Mr. Dupont, that all the words after "That" to the end of the question, be left out, and the words, "there are in our judicial system too great a number of tribunals having an appeal jurisdiction; that a.n humble address be presented to His Excellency, the Governor General in Council, praying him to communicate with the several provincial governments, with the object of arranging a judicial system which will diminish the number of appeal jurisdction6, whilst guaranteeing an efficient administration of justice equally satisfactory to all the provinces", inserted instead thereof.
And the question being put on the amendment; the house divided:

and it passed in the negative.
There are at least four Canadian precedents for the course which we are seeking t-o pursue under the amendment moved by the hon. member for Lake Centre. I suggest that the

Plebiscite Act-Point of Order
amendment does not -come within the ambit of tihe principle enunicated by the Prime Minister, and therefore it is in order. I do not wish to labour the point. In my mind at least, and I hope in the minds of legal gentlemen of the house, the point is clear. I could hardly expect that laymen would be quite so appreciative of the technicalities of the situation.

Mr. Speaker, I am not one of that enlightened company, the members of the legal profession, to which my hon. friend has alluded, and to Which he belongs, but I am a humble member of this house and like him I have perused the Journals of the House of Commons. I notice that my hon. friend has given us several citations from the Journals which run back a good many years, some of them fifty or more. Perhaps as a member of the legal profession as well as a member of this house he will be inclined to place more importance upon the more recent rulings given by former Speakers. Therefore, I would direct Your Honour's attention to rulings which have been made by fhe Speaker of the House of Commons in regard to similar motions and which support entirely the point of order which I raised at the time the hon. member introduced his amendment.
I would refer the house and Your Honour to the Journals of the House of Commons of Thursday, March 8, 1934, at page 156 as follows:
The order being read for the second reading of Bill No. 19, an act to incorporate the Bank of Canada;
Air. Rhodes moved,

That the said bill be now read the second time.
And a debate arising thereon;
Air. Coote, seconded by Air. Irvine, moved in amendment thereto: That all the words after "That" in the main motion be struck out and the following substituted therefor:
"in the opinion of this house the government should give further consideration to the matter of providing that the stock of the proposed central bank should be owned by the government, and that the governor and directors of said bank should be appointed by the governor in council."
And a debate arising thereon;
Air. Rhodes raised a point of order that the proposed amendment of the honourable member was out of order for the reason that it anticipated amendments embodying the principle which may be submitted in committee on the bill.
After debate thereon;
Air. Speaker declared the said proposed amendment out of order for the reason that it deals with the provisions of the bill upon which it is moved in that it provides that stock in the bank should be owned by the government, whereas the bill by section 16 provides that the [Mr. R. B. Hanson.}
stock shall be allotted to the public with certain restrictions in regard to chartered banks and foreigners. The amendment proposes that the governor and directors should be appointed by the governor in council, whereas the bill by sections 8 and 9 provides that the directors be appointed by the shareholders. This is an amendment which may be moved in committee and not such an amendment as can be moved on a motion to read the bill a second time. Further, the amendment attaches conditions to the second reading of the bill in that it calls for further consideration of the matter set forth in the amendment before the bill is given second reading.
The debate on the second reading of said bill was then resumed.
That is precisely the position we are in at the moment with respect to the amendment of the hon. member for Lake Centre. I have taken exception to it on the score that while it is an amendment which may be moved in committee, it is not such an amendment as may be moved on a motion to read the bill a second time. Further, the amendment attaches conditions to the second reading of the bill in that it calls for further consideration of the matter set forth in the amendment before the bill is given second reading. That is one ruling of a former Speaker of this house.
There is another ruling which perhaps I have even stronger reasons for recalling in that apparently while in opposition I had managed to get into the same box as my hon. friend and had moved an amendment which related to clauses in a bill which should instead have been considered when the bill was in committee. I had not taken exception to the principle of the bill and His Honour the Speaker ruled my amendment out of order in the following terms-
